---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/global/Breadcrumbs.astro';

const siteUrl = Astro.site ? Astro.site.toString().replace(/\/$/, '') : (import.meta.env.PUBLIC_SITE_URL || 'https://razonyasombro.com');
const currentURL = `${siteUrl}/en/shroud-of-turin/`;

const alternateLinks = [
  { hreflang: 'es', href: `${siteUrl}/ciencia-y-fe/sabana-santa-de-turin/` },
  { hreflang: 'en', href: `${siteUrl}/en/shroud-of-turin/` },
  { hreflang: 'x-default', href: `${siteUrl}/ciencia-y-fe/sabana-santa-de-turin/` },
];

const articleSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  mainEntityOfPage: {
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    '@id': currentURL,
  },
  headline: 'The Shroud of Turin: STURP, Carbon-14, and What Science Actually Says',
  description: 'Forensic and documentary investigation of the Shroud of Turin: STURP findings, blood chemistry (AB male), the 1988 Carbon-14 dating debate, 2026 microscopic sample review, and 2022 WAXS X-ray models.',
  image: [`${siteUrl}/og-default.jpg`],
  url: currentURL,
  datePublished: '2026-01-01T00:00:00Z',
  dateModified: '2026-10-28T00:00:00Z',
  author: [
    {
      '@type': 'Organization',
      name: 'Reason & Wonder Research Team',
      url: `${siteUrl}/autor/`,
    },
  ],
  publisher: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'Reason & Wonder',
    url: `${siteUrl}/en/`,
    logo: {
      '@type': 'ImageObject',
      url: `${siteUrl}/favicon.svg`,
    },
  },
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Shroud of Turin: STURP, Carbon-14, and Modern Scientific Studies | Reason & Wonder"
  description="Forensic and documentary investigation of the Shroud of Turin: STURP findings, blood chemistry (AB male), the 1988 Carbon-14 dating debate, 2026 microscopic sample review, and 2022 WAXS X-ray models."
  lang="en"
  ogType="article"
  alternateLinks={alternateLinks}
  alternateUrl="/ciencia-y-fe/sabana-santa-de-turin/"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(articleSchema)} />

  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-8 sm:py-12">
    <Breadcrumbs
      crumbs={[
        { label: 'Science & Faith' },
        { label: 'The Shroud of Turin' }
      ]}
    />

    <header class="mt-6 mb-10 space-y-4">
      <span class="inline-flex items-center px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-semibold bg-amber-500/10 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-500/20">
        Forensic Science &middot; Peer-Reviewed Investigation
      </span>
      <h1 class="font-serif text-3xl sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-950 dark:text-stone-100 leading-[1.15]">
        The Shroud of Turin: STURP, Carbon-14, and Scientific Studies
      </h1>
      <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 font-reading leading-relaxed">
        An objective synthesis of the 1978 STURP physical-chemical examination, anatomical trauma matching Roman crucifixion, human blood analysis (AB male), the 1988 Carbon-14 dating debate, the 2026 microscopic sample re-evaluation, and 2022 WAXS X-ray cellulose aging models.
      </p>

      <div class="pt-2 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 text-xs text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 border-t border-stone-200 dark:border-slate-800">
        <span>Research Dossier</span>
        <span>&middot;</span>
        <span>Peer-Reviewed Bibliography</span>
        <span>&middot;</span>
        <a href="/ciencia-y-fe/sabana-santa-de-turin/" class="text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 underline underline-offset-2">
          Leer versión en Español &rarr;
        </a>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- Key Scientific Findings Factsheet -->
    <div class="mb-12 p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-2xl bg-stone-100/80 dark:bg-slate-900/80 border border-stone-200 dark:border-slate-800 space-y-4">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-xl font-bold text-slate-950 dark:text-stone-100">
        Summary of Documented Forensic Findings
      </h2>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-xs sm:text-sm">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <span class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 font-medium">Textile Material:</span>
          <p class="font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-stone-100">Flax linen, 3:1 herringbone twill weave (4.4 × 1.1 m)</p>
        </div>
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <span class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 font-medium">Image Mechanism:</span>
          <p class="font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-stone-100">Superficial oxidation of primary cellulose layer (top 0.2 microns); zero pigments, binders, or capillary soak</p>
        </div>
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <span class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 font-medium">Blood Analysis:</span>
          <p class="font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-stone-100">Human male blood (XY), AB group, elevated bilirubin consistent with extreme acute physical trauma</p>
        </div>
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <span class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 font-medium">Chronological Status:</span>
          <p class="font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-stone-100">Debated: 1988 C-14 dating (1260–1390 AD, Damon et al., 1989; Arizona sample verified without mending by Freer-Waters &amp; Jull, 2026) vs. 2022 WAXS structural aging model compatible with 1st-century AD linen (conditional on secular thermal history)</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Article Content Body -->
    <div class="prose prose-stone dark:prose-invert max-w-none space-y-8 font-reading text-slate-800 dark:text-stone-200 leading-relaxed">
      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950 dark:text-stone-100">
          1. The 1978 STURP Project: Physical and Chemical Analysis
        </h2>
        <p>
          In October 1978, the Shroud of Turin Research Project (STURP)&mdash;a multidisciplinary team of over thirty leading American scientists, including specialists from Los Alamos National Laboratory, NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory, and the US Air Force Academy&mdash;was granted 120 continuous hours of direct access to the cloth to perform non-destructive spectroscopic, chemical, radiographic, and microscopic examinations.
        </p>
        <p>
          The official STURP final report, published in 1981 following dozens of peer-reviewed articles in journals such as <em>Applied Optics</em>, <em>Science</em>, and <em>Analytica Chimica Acta</em>, reached several definitive conclusions:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-sm">
          <li><strong>Absence of Artistic Medium:</strong> No paints, pigments, dyes, stains, or organic binders (such as egg white, animal collagen, or oils) account for the body image.</li>
          <li><strong>Extreme Superficiality:</strong> The image resides exclusively on the very crowns of the top linen fibrils. It has a depth of approximately 200 nanometers (the thickness of the primary cell wall of a single flax fiber), without penetrating the core of the yarn or bleeding through to the backside of the cloth.</li>
          <li><strong>True 3D Spatial Information:</strong> Unlike artistic drawings or ordinary photographs, which reflect directional lighting and perspective, the intensity variations on the Shroud are mathematically proportional to the precise physical distance between the cloth and the body surface. When scanned using the VP-8 Image Analyzer, an undistorted three-dimensional relief of a human form emerges.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950 dark:text-stone-100">
          2. Forensic Pathology and Anatomical Wounds
        </h2>
        <p>
          Forensic pathologists examining the Shroud, including Dr. Pierre Barbet (Chief Surgeon at Saint Joseph's Hospital, Paris), Dr. Robert Bucklin (Deputy Medical Examiner of Los Angeles County), and Dr. Pierluigi Baima Bollone (Professor of Forensic Medicine at the University of Turin), discovered clinical anatomical details that were completely unknown to medieval science and art:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-sm">
          <li><strong>Crucifixion at the Wrists (Destot's Space):</strong> The nail exit wounds are located in the wrist (the Space of Destot) rather than the palms. Biomechanical experiments demonstrated that driving nails through the palms cannot support the weight of an adult human body, causing the metacarpal bones to tear free. Piercing Destot's space damages the median nerve, mechanically compelling the thumb to flex inward into the palm&mdash;precisely as depicted on the Shroud, where both thumbs are invisible.</li>
          <li><strong>Roman Flagellation:</strong> Over 100 dumbbell-shaped contusions and lacerations cover the back, chest, and legs, perfectly matching the design of the Roman <em>flagrum taxillatum</em>, a whip equipped with paired lead weights or bone pieces. The trajectories indicate two distinct executioners of unequal height scourging the victim from opposite angles.</li>
          <li><strong>Cranial Wounds:</strong> Rather than a neat decorative circlet often pictured in Western art, the scalp exhibits puncture wounds covering the entire top and back of the head, consistent with a tight-fitting cap of sharp thorns (<em>Paliurus spina-christi</em>).</li>
          <li><strong>Pleural Puncture and Post-Mortem Flow:</strong> A lance wound in the right thorax matches the shape of a Roman <em>lancea</em>. The fluid outflow shows clear separation of red erythrocyte clots and clear serum fluid, matching the biblical description in John 19:34: <em>«immediately blood and water flowed out»</em>.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950 dark:text-stone-100">
          3. Blood Chemistry: Human, Male, and Traumatized
        </h2>
        <p>
          Biochemists Dr. John Heller and Dr. Alan Adler proved that the blood stains are not pigment:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-sm">
          <li>Positive identification of hemoglobin derivatives (hemochromogen, methemoglobin, porphyrins, and bile pigments).</li>
          <li>Immunoassay confirmation of human albumin and immunoglobulin (IgG).</li>
          <li>Serological typing confirming blood group <strong>AB</strong>, the same rare blood group identified in verified Eucharistic miracles (Lanciano, Buenos Aires).</li>
          <li>High concentration of bilirubin, consistent with a victim who underwent extreme systemic trauma, severe dehydration, and mechanical shock before death.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950 dark:text-stone-100">
          4. The Carbon-14 Dating (1988), the Reweaving Debate, and X-ray Structural Models (WAXS)
        </h2>
        <p>
          The chronological age of the Shroud remains the subject of rigorous debate in the scientific literature:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-3 text-sm">
          <li>
            <strong>The 1988 Radiocarbon Study (Damon et al., Nature 1989):</strong> Three accelerator mass spectrometry (AMS) laboratories (Oxford, Zurich, and Tucson/Arizona) dated sub-samples from a single corner strip (the Raes corner) to between <strong>1260 and 1390 AD</strong> with 95% confidence, concluding the textile was of medieval origin.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The Reweaving Hypothesis (Rogers, 2005):</strong> STURP chemist Dr. Raymond Rogers (<em>Thermochimica Acta</em>) argued that the sampled corner was unrepresentative because it had undergone 16th-century French "invisible reweaving" with dyed cotton threads following the 1532 Chambéry church fire.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Microscopic Re-examination of 1988 Aliquots (Freer-Waters &amp; Jull, npj Heritage Science 2026):</strong> In January 2026, researchers at the University of Arizona AMS Laboratory re-examined the remaining reserve fibers from their 1988 C-14 testing using optical, polarized, and epifluorescence microscopy. They found <em>no spliced cotton threads</em> and <em>no alizarin dye or reweaving</em> in that specific aliquot, supporting the analytical validity of the 1988 measurement on that sample, while the broader question of whether the perimeter strip is representative of the whole cloth remains actively discussed.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Wide-Angle X-ray Scattering (WAXS, De Caro et al., 2022):</strong> Researchers at Italy's National Research Council (CNR) applied Wide-Angle X-ray Scattering to measure the natural sub-microscopic degradation of cellulose chains in a Shroud yarn. Their results showed a degradation profile compatible with 1st-century AD linen (such as archaeological textiles from Masada, ~73 AD). However, the authors explicitly noted that this model is strictly contingent on the cloth's assumed secular thermal history (~22°C and 55% relative humidity over two millennia), serving as an experimental method rather than absolute chronological proof.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950 dark:text-stone-100">
          5. Scientific Prudence and Catholic Theological Understanding
        </h2>
        <p>
          Modern physics and optics have not yet succeeded in fully reproducing the complete set of physical, chemical, and three-dimensional characteristics of the Shroud image. Experimental hypotheses, such as vacuum ultraviolet (VUV) radiation pulses explored by Italy's ENEA research center, illustrate the complexity of the mechanism.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the Catholic Church, the Shroud is not an article of faith or dogmatic dogma, nor does the reality of Christ's Resurrection depend on forensic proof. The Church promotes honest, independent scientific research while venerating the Shroud as a profound, contemplative icon of the Passion and sacrificial love of Jesus Christ.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Sources & Academic Bibliography -->
      <section class="pt-8 border-t border-stone-200 dark:border-slate-800 space-y-4">
        <h3 class="font-serif text-lg font-bold text-slate-950 dark:text-stone-100">
          Selected Peer-Reviewed Bibliography
        </h3>
        <ol class="text-xs space-y-2 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-400 list-decimal list-inside">
          <li>Damon, P. E., Donahue, D. J., Gore, B. H., et al. (1989). <em>Radiocarbon dating of the Shroud of Turin</em>. Nature, 337(6208), 611-615. doi:10.1038/337611a0.</li>
          <li>Freer-Waters, R., &amp; Jull, A. J. T. (2026). <em>Microscopic and spectroscopic examination of linen fibres from the Turin Shroud radiocarbon sample</em>. npj Heritage Science, 14, art. 42. doi:10.1038/s40494-026-02530-7.</li>
          <li>De Caro, L., Sibillano, T., Lassandro, R., Giannini, C., &amp; Fanti, G. (2022). <em>X-ray Dating of a Turin Shroud’s Linen Sample</em>. Heritage, 5(2), 860-870. doi:10.3390/heritage5020047.</li>
          <li>Rogers, R. N. (2005). <em>Studies on the radiocarbon sample from the Shroud of Turin</em>. Thermochimica Acta, 425(1-2), 189-194. doi:10.1016/j.tca.2004.09.028.</li>
          <li>Heller, J. H., &amp; Adler, A. D. (1981). <em>A Chemical Investigation of the Shroud of Turin</em>. Canadian Society of Forensic Science Journal, 14(3), 81-103.</li>
          <li>Jumper, E. J., Adler, A. D., Jackson, J. P., et al. (1984). <em>A Comprehensive Examination of the Various Stain Characteristics on the Shroud of Turin</em>. Archaeological Chemistry III, ACS Advances in Chemistry, 205, 447-476.</li>
          <li>Bucklin, R. (1982). <em>The Legal and Medical Aspects of the Trial and Death of Christ</em>. Medicine, Science and the Law, 10(1), 14-26.</li>
        </ol>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
